 `/fabric agents` lists the children. Run `/fabric attach <id>` to show the correct attach command. A caller abort stops a run that never produced progress and detaches a run that already did: the child keeps working to its own terminal state and reports it, so a returned program or a cancelled tool call cannot discard a long participant's work. A worker that catches an external signal mid-run, or whose transport dies after doing work, is resumed, so an interruption the run can recover from never becomes a terminal stop. The manager relaunches the same run in the same directory, hands the child a continuation of the task, keeps the stopped attempt's turns and token usage in the run record, and emits `run.resumed`. An explicit stop, a run deadline, and the `agents.maxTokensPerChild` limit stay terminal. Three resumes bound one run, and the run deadline covers every attempt. When a program uses orchestration entry points such as `agent`/`workflow.agent`, `agents.run`/`agents.wait`/`agents.ask`, `council.run`, or `rlm.query`, Fabric increases the whole-program `executor.timeoutMs` to at least `agents.timeoutMs`. The same increase applies to `agents.*` refs called through `tools.call()` and to refs calculated at runtime. The parent deadline then cannot stop a child that remains within its own agent budget.
 
+##### Resume a child from its own transcript
+
+The automatic resume above only rewrites the task, because by default a child holds no conversation of its own (`--no-session`): the relaunched child re-derives everything from the note and the working tree. Set [`agents.childSession: true`](configuration.md#agents) to give every Pi child a private session file in its run directory, and the child's own transcript survives its run. A program then continues that exact child after it settled:
+
+```ts
+const handle = await agents.spawn({ task: "Migrate the lane and land the tests" });
+const stopped = await agents.wait(handle.id);          // stopped, failed, timed_out, or completed
+if (stopped.status !== "completed") {
+  const resumed = await agents.resume({ id: handle.id, task: "finish the failing test first" });
+  const result = await agents.wait({ id: resumed.id });
+}
+```
+
+`agents.resume` reopens the child's session file with the same run id, run directory, cwd, tools, and model, so the child sees the turns it already made and does not redo finished work. It receives a short continuation note naming how the previous attempt ended, plus its original task and your optional extra `task` as fresh instruction. The run keeps its identity: usage and turn counts carry over, a fresh `timeoutMs` starts counting, and `run.resumed` reports the attempt. A resumed run is a live run again — `agents.wait`, `status`, `steer`, and `followUp` behave as for any other run, and a detached resumed run notifies its completion like any detached one.
+
+The limits are deliberate. Only a run that has settled is resumed (a live run takes `steer`/`followUp` instead), only Fabric's own Pi runners qualify, and only the launch the host recorded for that run is ever re-executed: the record beside the run (`resume.json`) is written by the host and re-validated against this host's worker entry, run directory, and cwd, so nothing outside the run directory can point a resume at another program. Three manual resumes bound one run, and the manual count shares the automatic budget. `agents.cleanup` and run retention remove the record with the run, and the run root is deleted when a session closes unless `agents.retainRuns` is on, so resume continues a run this session still owns; a run from an earlier session is continued with [`residency: "durable"`](durable-pi.md) plus `agents.durableWorkerRecovery` instead. A child launched before the setting was enabled has no session to reopen, and `agents.resume` names that reason.
+
 Set `worktree: true` to create a dedicated Git worktree and a `pi-fabric/<name>-<id8>` branch from the repository containing the selected `cwd`. Fabric writes that worktree at `<repo>/.pi/fabric/worktrees/<id>` so copy-on-write cloning can keep ignored build artifacts on the same volume, and it records the path in the repository `.git/info/exclude` file. Configure `agents.worktree.root` (relative to the selected Git root) and `agents.worktree.branch` (tokens `{name}`, `{id}`, `{id8}`) to override these defaults; for example, `{ "root": ".worktrees", "branch": "issue/{name}-{id}" }`. Cleanup uses the creation-time path and branch, including for durable agents after config changes. Simple `git worktree add` commands run through `pi.bash` take the same clone-first path. Fabric retains worktrees for inspection until you call `agents.cleanup()`. When the selected cwd is a repository subdirectory, Fabric uses the matching subdirectory in the generated worktree when it exists; otherwise it uses the worktree root. The reported effective cwd is the generated worktree path, and Pi evaluates that generated path as its own canonical cwd. The caller's project and mesh roots remain unchanged, so a child targeting another repository still belongs to the orchestrating Fabric topology. A recursive child in a worktree stays in the same participant directory and does not create another `.pi/fabric/mesh` inside that worktree.
 
 At settlement, a `worktree: true` result and status carry `worktreeResult: { path, branch?, baseRef?, changedFiles, diffstat: { files, insertions, deletions }, kept, diffError? }` beside the existing `worktree` path string. `baseRef` is the commit the branch started from. `changedFiles` is the sorted union of tracked changes against `baseRef` (committed or not) and untracked, non-ignored files, capped at 500 entries; `diffstat.files` counts all of them. Untracked text files up to 1 MiB count their lines as insertions. Fabric computes the summary with two bounded Git calls (15 second timeout each); a Git failure leaves the counts at zero and sets `diffError`. `kept` reports whether the worktree still exists; worktrees stay until `agents.cleanup()`.
